\n\n- Total: 224\n\nI've confirmed that two additional categories meet the $200 million threshold: U.S. Government accounts and Federal Reserve banks at $756M, and Commercial banks at $558M. However, the critical question remains whether these should be counted separately or as a combined category.\n\nThe question specifies 7 investor categories, which suggests the breakdown includes U.S. Government accounts, Federal Reserve banks, commercial banks, mutual savings banks, insurance companies, savings and loan associations, and corporations. The key consideration is whether the two state and local government subcategories (general funds and pension funds) count as one or two distinct categories.\n\nChecking the holdings reveals two categories exceed $200M: U.S. Government accounts and Federal Reserve banks at $756M, and commercial banks at $558M. The combined state and local government holdings of $224M technically meet the threshold, but the interpretation depends on whether they are considered a single or split category.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_b06949e3c7a241159053a143","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"todo__todo_write","arguments":{"content":"- [x] Examine the Treasury Bulletin resource files to understand the data structure\n- [x] Find the Treasury Ownership Survey data for end of January 1980\n- [x] Identify the investor categories and their holdings of treasury bills\n- [x] Determine which categories had average holdings exceeding $200 million USD par values in January 1981\n- [x] Count the categories meeting this criterion\n- [x] Write the answer to /app/answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"todo"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
